{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788202482,"nonce":"pZml3tQBmUvuVtv/RZSCsRcAFCQ=","message":"VERIFICATION (backlog verification pass, git-bug 2f2796e, run fbb2c59)\n\nCORRECTED — two of the three references in the Context block do not resolve the\nway a worker will look for them, and the premise sentence is true in substance\nwhile being contradicted by the labels on the very issues it points at. The scope\nitself is sound and the audit it asks for is already half-done by evidence\nsitting on those issues.\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTION 1 — THE TWO CITED MOD ISSUES ARE **CLOSED** AND LABELLED\n`state:done`, WHILE THE WORK THEY DESCRIBE IS GENUINELY UNVERIFIED\n=====================================================================\n\nThe Goal says these behaviours are \"currently sitting in 'awaiting in-game\nverification'\". Substantively TRUE. But:\n\n- Factions `6072bac` — \"Verify recurring-regulars features (travel cooldown,\n  bond-weighted returners, reunion trips)\" — is `[closed]`, labels\n  `priority:p1, state:done, type:verification`. Its closing comment says, in as\n  many words: *\"Closing per Dorian: shifting focus to the through-traffic/silk-road\n  work (e6a3150). No code changes needed here — this stays a manual in-game\n  verification pass (F1/F2/F3 checklist above), pick it up whenever there's a play\n  session to spare.\"*\n- Guests `c6b935d` — \"Verify returner-majority stay bonus (linger 2x, no\n  pass-through, evening overnight hold)\" — same shape: `[closed]`,\n  `state:done, type:verification`.\n\nSo both were closed for triage hygiene, not because verification happened. Two\nconsequences for a 5.2 worker, and both cost real time:\n\n  (a) `git-bug bug --status open` in Factions or Guests **will not find them**.\n      The spec's Context block says \"read first\" and the default listing hides\n      both. Say `--status closed` or give the ids with the word CLOSED beside\n      them.\n  (b) `state:done` reads as \"verified\". A worker who trusts the label will\n      conclude 5.2's premise is stale and either narrow the scope or ask. It is\n      not stale; the label is.\n\n**And the closing comment is a gift the spec does not collect.** `6072bac`'s\ncomment #0 is a ready-made F1/F2/F3 acceptance checklist that names the exact\ndebug actions to force each case — `ForceReturner`, `'Roster: clear travel\ncooldowns'`, `'Dispatch: schedule reunion visitor now'`, the roster dump's \"on\ncooldown ~2x trip + buffer\" line, the dispatch log's pick+weight entries, the\nlanding log's \"X returner(s), Y new face(s)\", and `SpawnAudit`. That IS the audit\nthe scope bullet asks for (\"3.1 + whatever debug hooks Factions/Guests already\nship — AUDIT them first\"), already performed, sitting on a closed issue. Lift it\ninto the spec rather than re-deriving it.\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTION 2 — \"memory: recurring-regulars-feature\" IS NOT IN THIS PROJECT'S\nMEMORY\n=====================================================================\n\nThe file exists, at\n`/home/dorian/.claude/projects/-home-dorian-projects-rimworld/memory/recurring-regulars-feature.md`\n— the WORKSPACE project's memory, not autorimmer's. An agent working in\n`autorimmer` has `bench-window-on-workspace-3.md`, `bench-prefs-resolution-for-watching.md`\nand `terse-responses.md` in its own memory dir and nothing else; the referenced\nfile is invisible to it. (There is also a related\n`-home-dorian-projects-rimworld-Guests/memory/recurring-guests-backlog.md`.)\n\nCite the absolute paths in the spec. A memory reference that only resolves from a\ndifferent working directory is a dangling pointer, and this is the second one in\nthe Context block.\n\n=====================================================================\nCONFIRMED\n=====================================================================\n\n- The bench carries Factions, Guests and Hospitality (`_RimWorld-Agent/Mods/`).\n  CONFIRMED — the dependency \"Factions/Guests on bench (v1 modlist)\" is met.\n- `_mp/actions.json` exists and contains Factions and Guests entries. CONFIRMED.\n- 3.1's `dev:incident` is shipped (`[Verb(\"dev:incident\")]` in\n  `Source/AutoRimmer/DevVerbs.cs`). CONFIRMED — the forcing dependency is met.\n- The only unmet dependency is 5.1 (`7c83d6c`), which is still open and has its\n  own problems (see the verification comment there — its acceptance depends on a\n  bench-version mechanism that does not exist).\n\n=====================================================================\nTHE AUDIT BULLET IS MORE THAN HALF ANSWERED, AND NOBODY HAS LOOKED\n=====================================================================\n\nThe scope says \"AUDIT them first; if forcing a visit/return isn't cleanly\npossible, file the needed test hook as an issue in the mod's own repo\". Two\nthings a worker should be told before spending a session on that audit:\n\n1. **Factions already ships instrumentation.** `Factions/Source/` contains\n   `FactionsLog.cs` and `SpawnAudit.cs`, among ~20 other subsystem files touched\n   since 2026-07-20. `6072bac`'s comment #1 records a deliberate \"recorder plan\"\n   (`e806f7c` core, `3f351ba` instrumentation, both now closed `state:done`) whose\n   whole premise was: *\"once instrumentation lands, one ordinary play session\n   answers F1/F2/F3 from the session log (cooldown violations are rule checks,\n   bond recurrence is a stats check over the logged draw weights, reunion rolls\n   are recorded with chance + outcome).\"*\n\n   **That is a description of 5.2's assertion substrate, written a month earlier,\n   for the same three behaviours.** If `FactionsLog` emits what that comment says\n   it emits, scenarios 2 and 3 become log assertions rather than statistical\n   inference over observed visits — a materially cheaper and much less flaky\n   design than the one this spec implies. The first thing the worker should do is\n   read `FactionsLog.cs` and `SpawnAudit.cs` and decide whether rwtest asserts\n   over the JOURNAL, over the Factions session log, or over both.\n\n   I did NOT verify what those files actually emit — I confirmed only that they\n   exist and that the plan comment exists. That is the audit, and it is one file\n   read away.\n\n2. **`rimworld-tools/_meta/RECORDER-CONTRACT.md` exists** and, by its name, is\n   likely the contract that recorder plan produced. It is not referenced anywhere\n   in this issue. Worth reading before designing the assertion layer.\n\n=====================================================================\nACCEPTANCE\n=====================================================================\n\n- \"≥3 meaningful scenarios green on the bench, including the smoke test\" —\n  testable; blocked only on 5.1.\n- \"The mutation check demonstrably catches its seeded regression\" — testable and\n  the strongest item here, because it is the only one that proves the suite has\n  power rather than merely passing. Keep it. One thing to pin: scope bullet 5\n  says \"seed a deliberate regression locally (uncommitted) and confirm exactly the\n  right scenario fails, then revert\" — \"then revert\" needs to be a stated\n  requirement of the CLOSING evidence, not a step, or a dirty Factions worktree\n  outlives the round. Given this workspace's build rules (a DLL built against a\n  mutated source must never be committed), add: the mutation is reverted AND\n  `Factions/Assemblies/Factions.dll` is confirmed unchanged before the round\n  closes.\n- \"Any punted behaviors filed as follow-up issues — nothing silently dropped\" —\n  testable as a review item.\n- \"M3: Dorian reviews the suite output and signs off (hard gate)\" — correctly\n  labelled as a human gate, same as 4.3's.\n\nTwo gaps in the open questions worth closing before dispatch rather than during:\n- \"How prior-visit state is seeded (save fixture vs mod hook vs long natural\n  run)\" — `6072bac`'s checklist answers it for F1 and F3 (`ForceReturner`,\n  `'Dispatch: schedule reunion visitor now'`, `'Roster: clear travel cooldowns'`).\n  For F2 it says \"engineer a bond (dev-set opinion / repeat visits)\", and\n  AutoRimmer has no opinion-setting verb — `dev:set-need` and `dev:set-skill`\n  exist, nothing for `Pawn_RelationsTracker` opinions. That is a concrete\n  test-hook gap and it is exactly what the scope bullet says to file. File it\n  against AutoRimmer (a `dev:set-opinion`), not against Factions, since it is a\n  bench capability rather than a mod behaviour.\n- \"what of slice-4d population-conservation invariants is cheap to assert\n  globally\" — `SpawnAudit.cs` in Factions is, by name, that check already\n  existing. Read it before writing a second one.\n\n=====================================================================\nWHAT I AM INFERRING, NOT CONFIRMING\n=====================================================================\n\n- That `FactionsLog.cs` / `SpawnAudit.cs` emit what the recorder plan described.\n  I confirmed the files exist and that both recorder issues are closed\n  `state:done`; I did not read either file, and \"closed state:done\" has already\n  proved unreliable on this pair of repos (Correction 1).\n- That a `dev:set-opinion` verb is the right home for the F2 seeding gap. That is\n  a recommendation.","files":null}]}